      "sizes": {
        "LoD/1.11": 117,
        "LoD/1.11b": 117,
        "LoD/1.12a": 117,
        "LoD/1.13c": 117,
        "LoD/1.13d": 117
      },
      "name": "ConvertFloatingPointTo64BitInteger",
      "signature": "ulonglong ConvertFloatingPointTo64BitInteger(void)",
      "calling_convention": "__stdcall",
      "return_type": "ulonglong",
      "comment": "Converts extended-precision floating-point value to 64-bit signed integer with proper rounding.\n\nAlgorithm:\n1. Load floating-point value from ST0 and extract integer part using FISTP (convert and store as qword)\n2. Reload converted integer as float10 and subtract from original to get fractional part\n3. Check if integer part is zero AND upper 31 bits are all zero (handles small values)\n4. If integer part nonzero or upper bits set: calculate rounding adjustment based on fractional part\n5. For negative numbers (EDX < 0): XOR lower dword with 0x80000000, add 0x7FFFFFFF, use ADC to propagate carry to upper dword\n6. For positive numbers: add 0x7FFFFFFF to lower part, use SBB to handle borrow from upper part\n7. Return 64-bit result in EDX:EAX\n\nParameters:\nST0: Extended-precision floating-point value (float10) to convert\n\nReturns:\nEDX:EAX: 64-bit signed integer result (EAX = lower 32 bits, EDX = upper 32 bits)\n\nSpecial Cases:\n- Rounding threshold is 0x7FFFFFFF (2^31 - 1) on the fractional part\n- Negative fractional parts use XOR with 0x80000000 to flip sign bit for magnitude comparison\n- If both integer part and upper 31 bits of high dword are zero, checks if high dword has any significant bits before performing full calculation",
      "name_source": "LoD/1.11",
      "method": "MNE",
      "index": "MNE:0be5866c35abc0fb59235d2363df563a",
