      "name": "GetProcessorCountForHyperThreading",
      "signature": "int GetProcessorCountForHyperThreading(void)",
      "calling_convention": "__stdcall",
      "return_type": "int",
      "comment": "Retrieves the logical processor count for systems with Hyper-Threading support\n\nAlgorithm:\n1. Initialize SEH (Structured Exception Handling) frame with size 0xc bytes\n2. Push exception frame size (0xc) onto stack\n3. Push exception handler function address (0x6fc37bb8) onto stack\n4. Call SEH prolog to set up exception handling frame (__SEH_prolog)\n5. Clear processorCount local variable at [EBP-0x1c] to 0\n6. Clear exception handler flag at [EBP-0x4] to 0\n7. Preserve XMM0 register state (MOVAPD XMM0,XMM1) for floating-point operations\n8. Set default processorCount = 1 at [EBP-0x1c] (assumes single processor by default)\n9. Jump unconditionally to cleanup_and_exit label (no CPUID executed in this version)\n10. Set exception handler flag = -1 (0xffffffff) at [EBP-0x4] for cleanup activation\n11. Load processorCount from [EBP-0x1c] into EAX for return value\n12. Call SEH epilog to clean up exception frame and restore original stack (__SEH_epilog)\n13. Return processorCount value in EAX register\n\nParameters:\nNone - function takes no parameters\n\nReturns:\nint - Logical processor count, defaults to 1. Returns 1 if CPUID execution fails or is not available.\n\nSpecial Cases:\n- Function uses Structured Exception Handling (SEH) to safely execute potentially unsafe CPUID instruction\n- Default return value is 1 (single processor fallback)\n- Only called from DetectCPUCapabilities when CPU reports Hyper-Threading capability (CPUID.1 ECX bit 28 set)\n- XMM0 register is explicitly preserved during execution to maintain SSE state\n- Exception frame size is 0xc bytes (3 DWORD stack locations)\n- Function is part of CPU capability detection sequence for Hyper-Threading detection",
